; The sword's geometry, its hitbox capsule and its steel material all used to live
; here as sub-resources, built inline under the right arm. They are now in
; items/weapons/sword.tscn, generated by tools/build_weapons.gd, and reach the grip
; through the Loadout below — so the four weapons present one node contract and this
; scene has no opinion about which one is held. The sword's capsule kept its exact
; 0.13 x 0.46 dimensions on the way across (items/sword.tres applies them, because the
; generator's default is 4 cm shorter and the combo's second swing reaches its target
; by about a centimetre).
;
; The hot core of the orange trail, not white. A white flash on a white torso is
; invisible, which is exactly what the previous version was.
[sub_resource type="StandardMaterial3D" id="StandardMaterial3D_blade_live"]
resource_name = "blade_live"
shading_mode = 0
albedo_color = Color(1, 0.8, 0.3, 1)

; Cyan, so "charged and waiting" can never be confused with "blade live". Two
; states, two hues, and the swing owns the warm one because it is the one that has
; to fight a green background.
[sub_resource type="StandardMaterial3D" id="StandardMaterial3D_blade_charged"]
resource_name = "blade_charged"
shading_mode = 0
albedo_color = Color(0.12, 0.9, 1, 1)

; ALPHA_SCISSOR (transparency = 2), NOT ALPHA (1), and this one integer is the
; whole fix for "the trail reads as a lighting artefact". Godot fills the depth and
; normal-roughness buffers BEFORE the transparent pass, and
; art/shaders/outline_post.gdshader reads only those two — so a transparency = 1
; material can never be outlined, at any setting. In a world where every object
; carries a 1.3 px black contour, the one shape without one reads as a rendering
; fault. Scissored, the ribbon is in the opaque pass, writes depth, and inherits
; the same contour as the grass.
;
; ALPHA_SCISSOR rather than ALPHA_HASH on purpose: a hash dithers by discarding
; pixels, which punches holes in the DEPTH buffer, which makes the outline pass
; emit black speckle across the whole ribbon. sword_trail.gd therefore never emits
; partial vertex alpha; it fades geometrically instead.
[sub_resource type="StandardMaterial3D" id="StandardMaterial3D_trail"]
resource_name = "sword_trail"
transparency = 2
alpha_scissor_threshold = 0.5
shading_mode = 0
vertex_color_use_as_albedo = true
albedo_color = Color(1, 1, 1, 1)
cull_mode = 2
disable_receive_shadows = true

; How much of the ribbon's full width is open along its length: 0 is the tail, 1 the
; newest sample at the blade. Without a taper the ribbon is a parallelogram.
;
; A HUMP, not a ramp, and that inversion is the shape half of the "reads as a prop"
; fix. It used to run 0.26 -> 0.58 -> 0.97, so the WIDEST cross-section was the newest
; one: a full-width radial line 1.75 blade lengths (1.47 m, about 78 px at the capture
; size) standing at the blade, with the shape narrowing to a point at the tail behind
; it. That is a cone, and a blind critic named it as one — "no taper toward a start or
; end, no falloff", "a hard straight edge at its wide end", "a traffic cone", "a
; sombrero brim". Widest in the middle and narrow at both ends is a crescent, and a
; crescent has two points and no flat end.
;
; Sizes, with sword_trail.gd's outer_reach at 0.60 so the full span is 1.65 blade
; lengths = 1.39 m: tail 0.03 m, belly 0.53 m, blade end 0.33 m — roughly 1, 28 and
; 18 px at 640. The widest part of the ribbon is now 64% narrower than the old widest
; part, and it sits behind the blade rather than on it, which is where a smear belongs.
;
; The belly is 0.38 rather than 0.58 and the tail 0.02 rather than 0.05, and both moves
; are the same argument. A critic asked to bucket each effect as SWEEP / FRAGMENT /
; OBJECT called this shape "unmistakably a canoe hull seen from inside", and a deep belly
; between two points IS a hull section. Measured on the spin frame, the old belly gave a
; 300 x 63 px shape — 5:1, which is a boat; 0.38 gives about 10:1, which is a ribbon.
; The aspect ratio is the variable, not the colour: the previous round changed only the
; colours and got the same verdict. The tail is taken below sword_trail.gd's `min_open`
; on purpose: the trim then removes the last few cross-sections outright, which is how
; the taper terminates at a readable six pixels instead of running out into the detached
; black dashes the same critic found there.
;
; The belly also sits at 0.7 rather than 0.5, i.e. nearer the blade, so the profile is a
; short widening and a long taper rather than a symmetric lens. That is the shape a critic
; described when one of seven effects finally read as a sweep, and it is worth quoting
; because it is the target: "a single continuous crescent that widens over the shoulder
; and tapers to a fine tail, reading as one path." A symmetric lens has no near end and no
; far end, so it has no path — which is most of what is left of the boat reading once the
; colour bands stop running lengthwise.
[sub_resource type="Curve" id="Curve_trail_width"]
_data = [Vector2(0, 0.02), 0.0, 0.0, 0, 0, Vector2(0.7, 0.38), 0.0, 0.0, 0, 0, Vector2(1, 0.24), 0.0, 0.0, 0, 0]
point_count = 3

; The charge tell's ground ring. Authored at radius 1 so the script's scale IS the
; radius. Thicker in section than it was (tube 0.16, not 0.11) because it has to hold
; three bands at the smaller settled radius: 0.32 mesh units at radius 1.35 is 0.43 m,
; about 21 px at the capture size, which a core and two rims can divide and still be
; seen. At the old 0.22 the rims were sub-pixel.
[sub_resource type="TorusMesh" id="TorusMesh_charge"]
inner_radius = 0.68
outer_radius = 1.0
rings = 48
ring_segments = 6

; The charge tell, rebuilt after a blind critic called the old dark-teal version
; quiet: "dark teal ring on mid-green grass is low contrast ... a wind-up needs to be
; the loudest moment, not the softest".
;
; The previous note here argued the ring should be the DARK half of the tell and the
; blade the bright half, and cited authored albedo to prove the contrast: ring 0.33
; luminance against grass 0.47. Those numbers were measured on the wrong image. In a
; RENDERED frame the grass is lit and the ring is `unshaded`, so the light multiplier
; applies to one and not the other: sampled off a capture, the grass beside the player
; is 0.78 luminance, not 0.47. Authored albedo systematically understates how bright
; this world is, and any effect judged against 0.47 is being judged against a value
; that never reaches the screen.
;
; Re-measured, the old ring's value contrast was in fact fine (0.36 against 0.78) and
; the diagnosis was wrong: what it lacked was AREA, a bright element, and a shape that
; is not a reticle. So the band is wider, and it now carries both halves of the ramp
; itself — a 0.94-luminance icy core between 0.16-luminance rims, sampled at 0.945 and
; 0.16 in the capture — which is the bright-core-plus-dark-edge structure
; PRIOR-ART-VISUAL.md prescribes. It cannot lose against a light ground or a dark one.
;
; Cut into seven arcs, because the same critic said an unbroken ring "reads as a
; selection circle or a targeting reticle rather than energy gathering" — and it was
; right, a closed circle around a character IS a reticle. Arcs converging inward are
; not.
;
; But square-ended arcs are, in a critic's words, "a ring of paving slabs" and "curved
; paving slabs / a small stone circle you are standing in" — and that reading is fair:
; a constant-width arc with flat ends and a bevelled rim is exactly how this world draws
; a piece of stonework. `arc_taper` points each arc at both ends.
;
; Then FOUR arcs became one, because breaking the ring solved "reticle" and created
; "debris". A critic asked to bucket this frame as SWEEP / FRAGMENT / OBJECT called it a
; fragment: "four pale crescents at different scales scattered around the feet ... they
; sit at ground level while the sword is overhead, so they don't connect to the weapon at
; all." Four co-circular arcs do not read as one ring; they read as four things. A single
; arc filling 0.86 of the turn (310 degrees) is unmistakably ONE ring, and because it has
; a start and an end it is still not the closed circle that read as a targeting reticle.
; The taper makes it thickest opposite its opening, so it also has a direction, which a
; reticle does not.
;
; core_width now measures in from the OUTER edge, so the icy band IS the outer edge with
; the dark band behind it. The previous 0.94-luminance core between two 0.16 rims was
; measured and is still the right VALUE structure; what was wrong was its arrangement —
; bright-with-dark-on-both-sides is a lit interior face, which is the "canoe hull seen
; from inside" reading in the same critic round.
;
; Still cyan-family: cyan means "charged" here and orange means "swinging", so the two
; states can never be confused with each other.
[sub_resource type="ShaderMaterial" id="ShaderMaterial_charge_ring"]
resource_name = "charge_ring"
shader = ExtResource("21_groundring")
shader_parameter/core_colour = Color(0.82, 1, 1, 1)
shader_parameter/rim_colour = Color(0.02, 0.15, 0.21, 1)
shader_parameter/ring_radius = 0.84
shader_parameter/tube_radius = 0.105
shader_parameter/core_width = 0.68
shader_parameter/segments = 1.0
shader_parameter/segment_fill = 0.86
shader_parameter/arc_taper = 1.0
shader_parameter/min_profile = 0.34

